$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The matrix</h2>
<p>The underlying case was registered at Khardah Police Station in August 2023 and combines matrimonial-offence provisions — Sections 498A and 406 IPC — with grave sexual-offence allegations under Sections 377, 376D and 354 IPC and Sections 4, 8 and 14 of the POCSO Act. By the time the bail application reached Court No. 8 of the Appellate Side, the petitioner had been in custody for 1,112 days — a shade over three years — and pressed that the trial had made no meaningful progress.</p>
<p>The de-facto complainant opposed the plea with a decisive fact: the victim's evidence was fixed for the second week of December 2026. The State produced the case diary.</p>

<h2>What the court weighed</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody length</strong><br>1,112 days — a factor that ordinarily builds pressure toward release under Article 21 jurisprudence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The victim's statement</strong><br>Recorded under Section 183 BNSS and considered alongside the medical report.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The trial calendar</strong><br>The single most important input: the victim's deposition was weeks away, not an indefinite horizon.</div>
</div>
<p>Against that matrix the court declined bail "at this stage", kept the State's memo of evidence on record, and built the renewal expressly into the order: the prayer may be revived immediately after the victim's evidence concludes.</p>

<h2>Why the timing logic matters</h2>
<p>Orders of this shape are common across High Courts in POCSO and sexual-offence matters, and they reflect a consistent judicial technique. Where the apprehension justifying custody is specific — influence over a child witness yet to depose — the court matches the restriction to the apprehension. It does not dismiss the application outright on the gravity of the charge, which would leave the accused to fight a fresh round from scratch months later; it fixes the renewal point at the moment the apprehension expires. For the defence, the order is less a defeat than a scheduled second hearing with better facts.</p>

<h2>What each side should do between now and the deposition</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Defence:</strong> ensure readiness for the December date — any adjournment attributable to the accused will undercut the renewed application; keep a certified copy of the order recording the renewal liberty.</li>
  <li><strong>Prosecution:</strong> produce the victim on the fixed date with support-person arrangements in place; repeated failures to examine the witness convert the tampering logic into a delay argument for the accused.</li>
  <li><strong>Both:</strong> after the deposition, the renewed bail plea will turn on what the evidence actually was — consistency, corroboration by the medical record, and the remaining witness list.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> When opposing bail near a scheduled victim deposition, complainant-side counsel should place the fixed date squarely on record — it is often the fact that decides the order. Defence counsel, conversely, should seek an express renewal liberty tied to the deposition rather than a bare dismissal: the difference determines whether the next application starts from a changed-circumstance platform or from zero.</p>
</div>
